WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.056 --> 00:00:04.055
The life of Mary Wollstonecraft Shelley began in tragedy.

2
00:00:04.056 --> 00:00:14.951
Born in London, England, on August 30, 1797, Mary lost her mother, the famous feminist Mary Wollstonecraft, to an infection 11 days after her birth.

3
00:00:14.952 --> 00:00:24.032
Although her father, the noted philosopher and novelist William Godwin, doted on Mary, she sorely missed the presence of her mother as she was growing up.

4
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:37.270
Despite such a lonely beginning in life, Mary, who, as one biographer wrote, "entered the world like the heroine of a Gothic tale," became a remarkable individual whose writing has had a lasting impact on the world.

5
00:00:37.271 --> 00:00:43.091
Although she was not formally educated, Mary learned to read and write at a very young age.

6
00:00:43.092 --> 00:00:51.926
With access to her father’s great library, she quickly became an independent scholar and writer, which was highly unusual for girls at the time.

7
00:00:51.927 --> 00:01:00.744
She enjoyed reading about a broad range of subjects and even had one of her own short stories published by her father’s company when she was only eleven.

8
00:01:00.745 --> 00:01:04.498
Nevertheless, Mary’s home life was not happy.

9
00:01:04.499 --> 00:01:11.580
Her father remarried, and her new stepmother preferred her own children to Mary and was not kind or loving to her.

10
00:01:11.581 --> 00:01:18.247
At the age of fifteen, Mary was sent to live at the home of William Baxter, a family friend, in Dundee, Scotland.

11
00:01:18.248 --> 00:01:25.817
The Baxter home had a profound effect on Mary because it was her first experience living in a family with a loving mother and father.

12
00:01:25.818 --> 00:01:30.810
On a visit home to London, Mary met a young poet named Percy Bysshe Shelley.

13
00:01:30.811 --> 00:01:41.047
Although Shelley was married and had a young daughter of his own, he became a frequent visitor at the Godwin family home during the next two years and struck up a deep friendship with Mary.

14
00:01:41.048 --> 00:01:48.048
They shared a passion for radical thinking and literature and a commitment to the ideals of freedom and democracy.

15
00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:54.960
In 1814, in a surprising turn of events, Shelley left his wife and daughter, eloping to France with Mary.

16
00:01:54.961 --> 00:02:08.288
Shocked and embarrassed, Mary’s father refused to speak to either of them for the next three years, until they were finally married on December 30, 1816, several months after Percy Shelley’s wife committed suicide.

17
00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:12.523
Between their elopement and marriage, the young couple moved around Europe.

18
00:02:12.524 --> 00:02:20.778
Mary followed academic pursuits, easily learning French as well as Italian, and reading many of the poets whose works had inspired Shelley.

19
00:02:20.779 --> 00:02:28.102
When their money began to run low, Shelley, determined to spend his life writing, was forced to go into hiding from his creditors.

20
00:02:28.103 --> 00:02:36.588
In March 1815, Mary sank into a depression when their first child, a daughter named Clara, died when she was only a few weeks old.

21
00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:45.323
Percy Shelley, who was involved in his own desires and goals, became distant, spending more time with his friends and fellow poets than with his wife.

22
00:02:45.324 --> 00:02:53.570
In 1816, however, things began to look up when Mary had a second child, a boy they called William, after her father.

23
00:02:53.571 --> 00:03:00.336
Shelley also began to have some success with his writing, which Mary often edited and prepared for publication.

24
00:03:00.337 --> 00:03:10.060
Oddly, it was during this happy time following William’s birth and the first major publication of Shelley’s poetry that Mary produced her bleak masterpiece.

25
00:03:10.061 --> 00:03:21.494
It was the summer of 1816, and the couple, along with their second child, the infant William, and Mary’s stepsister, Claire Clairmont, went to Geneva to spend time with Lord Byron, an influential poet. They

26
00:03:21.495 --> 00:03:26.046
rented a house on the shores of Lake Geneva and spent the days boating and writing. Every

27
00:03:26.047 --> 00:03:33.305
evening, they would meet at the house and share their writings. The gatherings were inspiring to each of them. That

28
00:03:33.306 --> 00:03:42.466
summer, Shelley wrote his "Hymn to Intellectual Beauty," a significant achievement, and Byron completed part of his "Childe Harold's Pilgrimage." Clairmont

29
00:03:42.467 --> 00:03:48.442
also wrote several poems, but Mary seemed unable to get her thoughts onto paper. Then,

30
00:03:48.443 --> 00:03:52.755
one evening, they were forced to stay inside because of a fierce storm. Byron

31
00:03:52.756 --> 00:03:57.022
suggested that they have a contest to see who could write the best horror story. Inspired

32
00:03:57.023 --> 00:04:02.782
by a dream in which she brought her baby back to life, Mary finally had a breakthrough. She

33
00:04:02.783 --> 00:04:10.338
began writing Frankenstein: or, the Modern Prometheus , a short novel that became her masterpiece.

34
00:04:10.339 --> 00:04:18.759
The story centers on Dr. Frankenstein, a scientist obsessed with solving the mysteries of nature and giving life to an inanimate body.

35
00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:24.734
He puts a man together out of body parts taken from a graveyard and uses science to bring him to life.

36
00:04:24.735 --> 00:04:31.146
But Frankenstein is appalled when his creation turns out to be hideous, not the beautiful man he had imagined.

37
00:04:31.147 --> 00:04:37.163
Dr. Frankenstein quickly abandons his beast, leaving him to fend for himself in the world.

38
00:04:37.164 --> 00:04:44.477
The lonely creature comes across a loving family who treat each other with kindness and respect, and envies their relationship.

39
00:04:44.478 --> 00:04:54.152
However, when the family rejects him because of his horrid appearance, the monster takes his revenge out on his creator, killing everyone the doctor loves.

40
00:04:54.153 --> 00:05:05.126
In the end, Dr. Frankenstein must take responsibility for his creation and chases him into the frigid Arctic, where he spends the rest of his life trying to catch his monster.

41
00:05:05.127 --> 00:05:17.954
This tragedy is reflected in the title; Prometheus was a Greek god who gave fire to humans and was forever punished for it—just as Dr. Frankenstein tried to use technology to create life and suffered for his ambition.

42
00:05:17.955 --> 00:05:25.303
Many literature experts believe that Mary Shelley’s story is a warning against the blind pursuit of technology that characterized her era.

43
00:05:25.304 --> 00:05:34.106
(The early nineteenth century saw the growth of factories, and the invention of railways, gas lighting, and steam engines, as well as a new disregard for nature.)

44
00:05:34.107 --> 00:05:42.023
On the other hand, many other writers consider Frankenstein to be a reflection of 19-year-old Mary’s own loneliness and desire for a family.

45
00:05:42.024 --> 00:05:49.269
Like the monster she created, Mary Shelley searched for companionship and love but found them elusive.

46
00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:59.593
By the time she was 25, Mary had witnessed the deaths of three of her four children (only one survived to adulthood) and her young husband, who drowned in a boating accident.

47
00:05:59.594 --> 00:06:08.410
After Percy Shelley’s death in 1822, Mary became a champion of his poetry, helping him achieve the great status that he has to this day.

48
00:06:08.411 --> 00:06:12.005
She also went on to write several more novels of her own.

49
00:06:12.006 --> 00:06:17.817
When Mary Shelley died in 1851, her Frankenstein had already become a classic.

50
00:06:17.818 --> 00:06:27.555
The novel, which some see as a simple horror story and others acclaim as one of the first works of science fiction, continues to inspire writers and filmmakers around the world.

51
00:06:27.556 --> 00:06:39.681
Whether in the modern literary genre of science fiction or in a Hollywood monster movie, the tale of Mary Shelley’s monster lives on as one of the most recognizable narratives in English literature.

